   "title": "ZHR",
   "problemType": "practical",
   "points": null,
   "topics": [
    "astronomy"
   ],
   "difficulty": "Hard",
   "importance": 2,
   "statement": "The visual observations of meteors are necessary to monitor the meteor showers of the high and intermediate activity. Despite the fact that the accuracy of the visual observations is much lower than that of the photometrical ones, the large number of observations have large significance for the statistical results.\n\nYou are provided by supplement papers: the log of the observations and the map of the gnomonic projection with listed meteor events.",
   "sections": [
    {
     "id": "part-1",
     "title": "1.",
     "statement": "Observations were organized in Sudak, the local time (LT) used. Please, write in Table 1 the time of the beginning ($T_b$) and the end ($T_e$) of observations using Universal time (UT)."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-2-1",
     "title": "2.1.",
     "statement": "The meteors that belong to the same shower (S) have one radiant. The name of the shower coincides with the name of the constellation where the radiant is situated.\n\nIndicate the place on the map where the radiant is situated (for Perseids). List in Table 2 the numbers of meteors (No) that are showed on the map that belong to this stream (see the supplement)."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-2-2",
     "title": "2.2.",
     "statement": "Determine and list in Table 2 the equatorial coordinates of the radiant ($\\alpha,\\delta$)."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-3",
     "title": "3.",
     "statement": "Write in Table 2 the date of the maximum of the meteor shower Perseids (in DD.MM format). Estimate (roughly with an accuracy 1h) the beginning ($T_b$) and the end ($T_e$) of the shower visibility during the night of the maximum."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-4",
     "title": "4.",
     "statement": "The calculation of the hour number of meteors (ZHR) for the Perseid shower.\n\nZHR is a measure of the shower activity. It can be defined as a number of meteors that are seen by observer during one hour of observations with the favourable conditions and with a radiant situated in zenith. The corresponding formula is:\n\n$$ZHR=\\frac{K^{6.5-lm}NF}{T_{\\rm eff}\\sin h_r}$$\n\n$K$ – coefficient of the luminosity function that is equal to 2.5,\n\n$lm$ – limiting stellar magnitude (each observer determines this value individually taking the most faint star he or she can observe),\n\n$N$ – observed number of meteors,\n\n$F$ – coefficient correcting the cloudiness of the sky,\n\n$T_{\\rm eff}$ – effective time of observations in hours,\n\n$h_r$ – the angular altitude of the radiant above the horizon."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-4-1",
     "title": "4.1.",
     "statement": "Calculate the mean value of the limiting stellar magnitude (lm) for each interval. Write your results in Table 3."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-4-2",
     "title": "4.2.",
     "statement": "Calculate effective time of observations ($T_{\\rm eff}$) for each interval. Write your results in Table 3.\n\nZHR corresponds to the ideal sky condition. If there is a haze in the sky, some clouds or some extra light (Moon, artificial light, twilights), the visual number of meteors will be decreased, because faint meteors can not be observed."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-4-3",
     "title": "4.3.",
     "statement": "Calculate the correction for the cloudiness for each interval ($F$).\n\n$$F=\\frac1{1-k}$$\n\nWhere $k$ is percentage of the cloudiness, $t$ is corresponding time interval in minutes, and $t_{\\rm total}$ is the total time of observation in a given interval.\n\n$$k=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i t_i}{100\\cdot t_{\\rm total}}$$\n\nWrite your results in Table 3."
    },
    {
     "id": "part-4-4",
